Yes, but Ignatius J. Reilly didn't comment on the fact that writing
systems may affect adoption. He talked about some innate flexibility
in English which he failed to specify, proove, or give references for
(but insists that he had all along:-).

Languages aren't even bound to their writing systems, btw, cf. eg.
China/Taiwan system (very different--I had a fried teach me some of it
once), Korean (South vs. North), Vietnamese (which used to be written
with Chinese characters), or indeed the simpler cases of the German
Schreibreform or the American/UK spelling differences.

If country X with language x with an awful writing system x' would
invades and stays in underdeveloped, disunite territory Y with
numerous languages y1, y2, y3 and a simple writing sytem y', people
will *have* to learn x to be able to communicate with the invaders,
because it will be in their interest. If it comes to adoption of x as
common language, it _may_ be that they don't adopt x' but use y'
instead. How likely that is will surely depend on the concrete
circumstances.

